A||||9||mbramble||Mecham Scoops - How best to use??||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||04-28-2001||09:40 AM||||I recently purchased a set of the Mecham(sp?) quarter panel/scoops with the strakes.  On the driver's side, I fabricated a plenum that each of the 3 air intake holes feeds into.  I then mounted the stock flexible gasket/hose that feeds into the air intake.  So, this panel feeds air directly into the stock intake system.  I'm satisfied with that.<P>Any suggestions on what to do on the passenger's side to best utilize the extra air that will be coming in??  I have relocated the battery up front so there is a big empty space there and you can see directly down to the back of the quarter panel (battery box rusted away).  Should I just leave it like that to allow cooling air to enter the engine compartment or should I fab up a plenum on that side and then direct the air to some specific part of the engine compartment??<P>Thanks,<BR>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Wannabe||04-28-2001||11:33 AM||||You might want to re-direct some of that air flow or else you could get lots of road crud building up on the front of your engine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Spektyr||04-28-2001||03:20 PM||||I'd recommend funnelling the air towards the belts on your motor.  It will significantly increas their lifespan.  Wouldn't hurt if you ran it through a lightweight filter, kind of like a furnace filter only smaller.  That'd keep the big ugly chunks out of the engine compartment.<P>Of course, I'm not an expert...<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||04-28-2001||08:41 PM||||If you want to extend you engine's life and aid in cooling you could add a oil cooler. Or, you could install a hose and direct the air to the A/C compressor or the alternator.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000004||FieroBUZZ||04-28-2001||08:57 PM||||I am making a set of NACA ducts (like on stock cars) to fit the quarter panels.    My battery is gone up front and the side wall at the back is rusted away.  <BR>On the passenger side I plan ducting the air into the engine compartment.  My wall repair will include ducting and a 'cover' panel to keep the wet away from the wires and flat floor.  I had planned to direct some air towards the alternator and to the front belt area.<P>Gary||||reg||12
Z||000005||mbramble||04-28-2001||09:18 PM||||How does an oil cooler work??  How is it connected to the oiling system??  Where do I get one??<P>Thanks,<BR>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||lou_dias||04-28-2001||11:33 PM||||put a fine screen to prevent road dust from ruining your alternator belt, that was the big change I noticed.  Ever since I got those scoops, I go through an alt per year and dirty engine compartment...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fiero_Freak||04-29-2001||11:48 PM||||Ditto on the oil cooler.  I also have a relocation filter kit and thats where my PH8 (mustang) filter is located.  You might be able to tie it into that?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||04-30-2001||12:28 AM||||And of course, the very best way to use the scoops is to install them on my car.  (Second best way would be to install them on <I>your</I> car.)<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||04-30-2001||07:44 AM||||An oil cooler is simply a radiator for your oil. Typically, you'd install a remote oil filter kit and tap into the return line (from the filter back to the motor) and install the cooler. Check out Summit (www.summitracing.com) or Jegs (www.jegs.com) for examples. Sometimes, if your lucky, you can find an old taxi cab or police car in the U-Pull-It yard and get one for real cheap.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
